        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why is my walking pad belt slipping?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most often because the deck has dried out, which drags on the belt under your foot so it feels loose. Lubricate as the manual says first. If it still slips, tighten both rear bolts equally, a quarter turn at a time, stopping as soon as the slip stops.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I tighten a walking pad belt?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Unplug it, mark both rear bolt heads, and turn each by the same quarter turn in the tightening direction your manual gives. Plug in and test with a firm step at a slow speed. Repeat only if it still slips, then check the belt is still centred.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my walking pad belt keep moving to one side?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually the tracking needs a small adjustment, the pad is not level, or you walk to one side of the belt because of where your desk is. Level the pad on a hard surface, centre it under your work area, then make a quarter-turn tracking correction on one bolt as the manual describes.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Which way do I turn the bolt to center a walking pad belt?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On many pads you turn the bolt on the side the belt is drifting toward, a quarter turn, but direction conventions vary between brands. Check your manual, make one small turn, and wait at least a minute before judging the result.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do auto-centering walking pad belts need adjusting?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Less often, but yes. Auto-centering helps with small wander, but it cannot correct a pad on an uneven floor or soft rug, a twisted frame, or a rear roller well out of line. Level the pad first, then adjust as the manual says.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it normal for a new walking pad belt to loosen?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A little, yes. New belts settle and lengthen slightly in their first hours of use, so one or two small tension and centring checks early on are normal. Needing re-tensioning every few days, or a belt that will not stay centred on a level floor, is worth raising with the seller.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>When should I replace a walking pad belt?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              When the edges are frayed, the underside is glazed or smooth, there are cracks or a lifting seam, or it has stretched so far the bolts are near the end of their travel. Order the model-specific belt from the brand where possible.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
